\section{Keeping keys out of messages}
\label{sec:redact-secrets}

Some APIs take their key in the URL: IEEE Xplore as [[apikey]], and
OpenAlex's rate-limit endpoint as [[api_key]].
A failed request names its URL in the exception message, so the key
went wherever the message went.
With a proxy that did not answer, [[scholar providers check]] printed
the probe's URL, key and all, in its table (issue~\#159); the same text
reaches the log and, through [[last_error]], a session file.

[[redact_secrets]] replaces the value of every query parameter whose
name says it is a key: [[api_key]], [[apikey]], [[api-key]] and
[[key]], in any case (so [[apiKey]] too).
It also hides [[mailto]], the contact address that OpenAlex and
Crossref take in the URL, and [[email]], where Unpaywall takes it: it
is not a secret, but it is personal data, and a failure message is
shown, logged and pasted into bug reports (issues~\#160 and \#259).
The name must start the parameter: [[monkey=1]] or [[keyword=x]] are
not keys and are left alone.
The value ends where a URL parameter ends, or at a quote, a bracket or
white space, since in a message the URL is followed by text.
The name stays, so the message still says which parameter was sent.

Redacting the message is the last line of defence, applied where a
message is shown or logged ([[check_provider]] and the providers that
put a key in a URL); keeping the key out of the URL in the first place
is better, where the API allows it (\cref{sec:openalex} for the
OpenAlex probe).
Keys sent in headers (Semantic Scholar, Web of Science, Scopus) never
appear in these messages: [[requests]] and [[urllib3]] name the URL in
their errors, not the headers.
Other libraries' messages can, though: an exception's text may print a
header mapping, \enquote{\{'x-api-key': '...'\}}, or an
\enquote{Authorization: Bearer ...} line, and [[request_failure_reason]]
passes the text of an exception it does not know on (redacted, below).
So the header and mapping forms are hidden too: a name among
[[x-api-key]], [[api-key]], [[api_key]], [[apikey]] and
[[authorization]], in any case, followed by [[:]] or [[=]] and a value,
bare or quoted, which may begin with a scheme word ([[Bearer]],
[[Basic]], [[Token]]) that is kept; and a bearer token wherever it
stands.  The name and the scheme word stay, so the message still says
what was sent.

<<secret redaction>>=
def redact_secrets(text: str) -> str:
    """Return `text` with keys, tokens and contact addresses hidden.

    Covers the URL parameters `api_key`, `apikey`, `api-key`, `key`,
    `mailto` and `email`, the header or mapping forms of `x-api-key`,
    `api-key`, `api_key`, `apikey` and `authorization`, and bearer
    tokens, in any case.
    """
    text = SECRET_PARAMETER.sub(rf"\1={REDACTED}", str(text))
    text = SECRET_HEADER.sub(rf"\1\2\3{REDACTED}", text)
    return BEARER_TOKEN.sub(rf"\1{REDACTED}", text)
<<constants>>=
SECRET_PARAMETER = re.compile(
    r"(?i)(?<![\w.-])(api[_-]?key|key|mailto|email)"
    r"=[^&\s'\"<>()\[\]{}]*"
)
SECRET_HEADER = re.compile(
    r"(?i)(?<![\w-])((?:x-)?api[_-]?key|authorization)"
    r"(['\"]?\s*[:=]\s*['\"]?)((?:bearer|basic|token)\s+)?"
    r"[^\s'\"&,;<>()\[\]{}]+"
)
BEARER_TOKEN = re.compile(r"(?i)\b(bearer\s+)[^\s'\"&,;<>()\[\]{}]+")
REDACTED = "[redacted]"
@

