\section{Saying why a request failed}
\label{sec:request-failure-reason}

The per-paper lookups of an enrichment run (the providers' DOI
lookups, Unpaywall, the Crossref check) return \enquote{undetermined}
on any failure, and since issue~\#259 they also leave a short reason,
which [[scholar enrich]] counts in a tally at the end of the run
(\cref{sec:enrich-lookup-tally}).
A reason is counted, so it must be the same for every paper the same
cause hit, and it is printed, so it must carry neither a key nor the
contact address.
An exception's own text meets neither demand when it comes from
[[requests]]: it names the request's URL, with the DOI and the query
parameters, the address among them for Unpaywall ([[email]]) and
Crossref ([[mailto]]).
[[request_failure_reason]] therefore puts such failures in words of
its own:
\begin{description}
\item[A status] becomes \enquote{HTTP 503}, or \enquote{rate limited
  (HTTP 429)} for the status that says to wait, the words the rate
  limiter and Semantic Scholar's retries use for the cooldowns they
  record ([[http_status_reason]]).
\item[Spent retries] of a session with a [[Retry]] (Crossref,
  Unpaywall) end in [[RetryError]], which [[HTTPAdapter.send]] raises
  for a [[MaxRetryError]] whose cause is an error answer.
\item[A connection] that failed or timed out (including [[SSLError]]
  and [[ProxyError]], which are [[ConnectionError]]s) is named by its
  class.
\item[Any other [[requests]] failure] (an invalid URL, a body that is
  not JSON) is named by its class alone.
\end{description}
Everything else keeps its text, on one line, redacted
(\cref{sec:redact-secrets}) and cut to [[FAILURE_REASON_MAX_CHARS]].
That is where the most useful reasons come from: an environment that
cannot make any HTTPS request at all, such as one without a CA
bundle, gets a plain [[OSError]] from [[HTTPAdapter.cert_verify]]
(\enquote{Could not find a suitable TLS CA certificate bundle, invalid
path: \dots}), before any URL is involved, and that text is what the
user needs to see, once per service, with the number of papers it
cost.

A limiter's refusal ([[RateLimited]]) is not handled here: this module
does not know the limiter, and each caller catches the refusal
separately anyway, taking its [[reason]].

<<failure reasons>>=
def http_status_reason(status: int) -> str:
    """Return the reason a request answered with HTTP `status` failed."""
    if status == 429:
        return "rate limited (HTTP 429)"
    return f"HTTP {status}"


def request_failure_reason(failure: Exception) -> str:
    """Return a short reason for a failed request, without its URL.

    The same cause gives the same reason for every request, and no key
    or contact address is quoted.
    """
    if isinstance(failure, requests.exceptions.RetryError):
        return RETRIES_SPENT
    if isinstance(failure, (requests.ConnectionError, requests.Timeout)):
        return f"connection failed ({type(failure).__name__})"
    status = getattr(getattr(failure, "response", None), "status_code", None)
    if isinstance(failure, requests.HTTPError) and isinstance(status, int):
        return http_status_reason(status)
    if isinstance(failure, requests.RequestException):
        return type(failure).__name__
    text = " ".join(redact_secrets(failure).split())
    if not text:
        return type(failure).__name__
    if len(text) > FAILURE_REASON_MAX_CHARS:
        text = text[:FAILURE_REASON_MAX_CHARS - 3].rstrip() + "..."
    return text
<<constants>>=
RETRIES_SPENT = "retries spent on HTTP 429 or 5xx answers"
FAILURE_REASON_MAX_CHARS = 120
@

